xli - an Xorg image display utility - since 1989

xli packages in many linux distros are based on a 2006 version.

version 1.18.0 beta has many patchs included, with multiple origins:
debian patches, netbsd patches, +35 other patches,
and also local changes.
see file CHANGES to see their descriptions.

plan is to release a 1.18 version.


changes in 1.18.0:
* exif rotation with jpg images
* webp support
* tiff support
* window symbol and title with standard wm hints
* -identify results consistency, easier to reuse
* toggle fullscreen while running with F11
* many others changes, see changelog


xli will display several types of images under X11, or load images onto
the X11 root window.
xli will display images also with pseudo-colors x11 servers display,
or even monochrome servers displays.


version 1.18.0-beta supports:
  Portable Network Graphics (PNG)
  GIF Image
  JFIF style jpeg Image
  WEBP Image
  Targa Image (TGA)
  Windows, OS/2 RLE Image (early BMP)
  Portable Bit Map (PNM, PBM, PGM, PPM)
  PC Paintbrush Image (PCX)
  X Pixmap XPM
  X Bitmap XBM
  X Window Dump XWD
  Tag Image File Format (TIFF)
  Sun Rasterfile RAS
  CMU WM Raster
  Facesaver Project (FS)
  G3 FAX Image
  FBM Image
  Utah RLE Image (RLE)
  GEM Image (IMG)
  MacPaint Image


A variety of options are available to modify images prior to viewing:
clipping, dithering, depth reduction, zoom (X, Y axis or both), 
brightening or darkening, input gamma correction, and image merging.  
When applicable, these options are done automatically (eg. a color image 
to be displayed on a monochrome screen will be dithered automatically).


while displaying the image, some keys can change the way it is displayed:
<, >     Zoom
r, l     rotate right, left
n, p     next/earlier image
5, 6     decrease, increase brightness
#, F11   fullscreen
esc, q   quit


SUGGESTIONS AND BUG REPORTS

xli has been developed in a fairly limited hardware environment, and
not all combinations of displays and/or image formats and options have
been tested. xli is useful to me, and maybe it will be useful to others.


